{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CROSS-REFERENCE — the zero-LLM root-cause analysis behind the Phase 3 hold now has a durable home: session log cosey-vinegarish-mouflon (d0d8-9dfe-f2d8-4364), entry 1 (\"Why the code-review severity axes are inert\"). It was originally written as a comment on the LLM-gate cost-analysis ticket childsafe-bizarre-lorikeet (comment 10, retained there), which the operator has ruled a scratchpad anti-pattern.\n\nPointer only — the analysis is unchanged. Its operative conclusion for this migration: the <=0.77% level-collapse flip rate is a CONFOUNDED measurement, because for 98.2% of scored findings blocking_enabled=False (only `security` is blocking-enabled) and the 0.95 non-security cut is functionally unreachable (7/432 attribute combos reach it, all requiring blast_radius=system AND likelihood=high AND reversibility=hard — a conjunction observed 0 times in 3,738 gradings; dead zone between 0.9175 and 0.95). Recommended sequence: P3.0 (zero-LLM) fix the threshold/aggregation and decide which criteria are blocking-enabled — an operator policy call — then P3.1 re-run code_decisiveness.py against the corrected decision rule; hold Phase 3 as written until then. The pending P3.0 blocking-lane decision references that analysis."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786588784378896001,"uuid":"d45fb770-c7f2-4432-b603-73f21d41eceb"}